---
id: deterministic-history
title: "Deterministic history after squash merges"
description: "An incremental export can survive rewritten monorepo history when its boundary is derived and every commit preserves complete identity metadata."
status: draft
series: git-splice
seriesOrder: 4
topics: [git, reproducibility, performance]
---

Pulling changes into a folder is only half of the problem. Pushing local changes back requires a history in which that folder becomes the repository root. `git subtree split` can build such a history, but for my workload it repeatedly walks too much of the monorepo.

The replacement must be incremental and deterministic.

## Derive the local boundary

The state file records upstream commit `U`, but deliberately stores no monorepo SHA. To find the local starting point, the tool searches current history for the newest commit that changed the folder's state file. Call that commit `B`.

A rebase may change the ID of `B`; a squash merge may replace several commits with one. Neither destroys the fact that some current commit introduced the recorded synchronization state.

There is one subtle case. The folder at `B` may not exactly equal the tree at `U`, because a squash merge can combine a pull and local edits. The export begins with a synthetic commit from `U` to the folder tree at `B`. Nothing is lost, and no stale monorepo identity is needed.

## Rebuild only what changed afterward

From there, the tool walks commits after `B` that touched the folder. For each one it writes the folder's tree as a root tree, removes the state file, maps parents, and creates a new commit with `git commit-tree`.

This changes the performance model. The first export may inspect substantial history. Later exports process only commits since the last synchronization boundary. Those two costs should be benchmarked separately; averaging them hides the property users care about.

## Determinism includes the committer

Copying the file tree is not enough to reproduce a Git commit ID. The rebuilt commit must preserve:

- author name, email, and date;
- committer name, email, and date;
- commit message;
- corresponding rebuilt parents.

`git-subrepo` copies author information but uses the person running the export as committer with the current time. Running the same rebuild twice therefore produces different IDs. That makes caching, comparison, and reasoning harder.

The useful oracle already exists. On histories where the new algorithm and `git subtree split` both apply, they should produce the same commit IDs. I do not need `split` in production to benefit from its years of edge-case handling; I can use it in tests to reveal disagreements.

This distinction—production dependency versus test oracle—has become one of my favorite design tools. A mature implementation can define expected behavior even when its operational constraints make it unsuitable for the final system.
